From f5001ce2f7a00d3924b23cceeb4b8b42258db5dc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 06:12:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] keep the item a press lands on selected when the press ends a
 rename
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

A press outside the field of a name being edited ends the edit and
keeps the name. The item is renamed at that moment, and its folder is
read again so that it shows under its new name, where it was then
selected, whatever was selected by then. The press itself went on to
what it landed on: on another item it selected that item, and a
right-click opened the item's menu. A moment later the folder had been
read, and the item just renamed was selected in its place, with the
menu still open. A menu's commands act on what is selected when they
are chosen, so Move to Trash under a menu that read Cut “Roadmap.md”
moved the file just renamed to the Trash, and so did Cmd+Backspace
after a plain click. The list and the icon view did this before this
branch. The column view has done it since what the window selects by
itself reaches its columns (7e8df53), in the first column and in a
further one, where a click on the folder's own row, a column back,
also ended with the item's column in use again. A name edited in the
sidebar's tree and ended on a file selected the folder among the
files, and the file's menu moved the folder to the Trash. An arrow key
pressed right after Return, before the folder had been read, was
undone the same way.

The reading that follows a rename now selects the item under its new
name only when no other item is selected among the rows as it ends.
That is asked before the rows are made again. Once they are, the old
name has left the selection, and the gallery, which always shows an
item, has selected its first one in its place: asking then whether
nothing is selected would leave the gallery on its first item after
every rename. In the column view the rows are the first column's, and
the item that counts is the one selected there while it is the column
in use, as the window selects in that column by itself only then. The
other views' selection can hold items that no column shows selected,
every item after Select All, and going by it would leave the item
unselected after Return.

So Return, a press on a bar or beside the items, and the gallery's
dialog end as they did, with the item selected under its new name. An
item inside the folder renamed is no other item either. A folder that
is open in the list is closed under its new name, and the row of an
item inside it goes with the old one, so the folder is selected as
before, where nothing would be.

A name edited in the sidebar's tree now leaves the items selected
among the files selected, after a press on one of them and after
Return alike: such a press selects nothing new, and can't be told from
Return by what is selected. The folder used to take their place. It is
still selected among the files when nothing else is.

A further column selects its renamed item again only when no other
item is selected there by then, and no column before it has become
the one in use, which selecting in it would undo. A column after it
showed what was in the item under its old name, and is opened again
for the new one. So a press on an item inside a folder whose name is
being edited, in the column to its right, still ends with the folder
selected and nothing selected in its column.

A Cmd- or Shift-click that ends an edit keeps what it selected, less
the item renamed, where only the item renamed used to stay selected.

Changelog (Fixed): Ending a rename with a click or a right-click on
another item selected the renamed item again a moment later. The menu
that the right-click had opened then acted on the renamed item, not on
the item it named, so its Move to Trash moved the file just renamed to
the Trash, and after a click the next key did the same. A name edited
in the sidebar's tree and ended on a file did this with the folder.
The item pressed now stays selected, an arrow key pressed right after
Return is no longer undone, and renaming a folder in the sidebar's
tree no longer takes the selection from the items selected among the
files.

